ime-of-the-ancient-mariner","targetName":"The Rime of the Ancient Mariner","context":"A contemporary concern that has been influenced by this poem's themes"},{"sourceSlug":"contemporary culture","sourceName":"Contemporary Culture","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-rime-of-the-ancient-mariner","targetName":"The Rime of the Ancient Mariner","context":"A lasting impact on popular culture and literature"}],"places":[{"name":"LONDON, ENGLAND","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"THE HEBRIDES ISLANDS","role":"Inspiration"},{"name":"AMSTERDAM, NETHERLANDS","role":"Publication location"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: The Rime of the Ancient Mariner","type":"Reference article","slug":"the-rime-of-the-ancient-mariner"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q708177","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Rime_of_the_Ancient_Mariner"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"The Rime of the Ancient Mariner contributed to the historical record of the Early Modern period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Rime of the Ancient Mariner is a major poem by the English Romantic poet Samuel Taylor Coleridge (1772–1834), first published in the joint collection Lyrical Ballads (1798) — co-authored with William Wordsworth and generally taken as the founding document of English Romanticism. In its final form (revised 1817, with marginal gloss added), the poem consists of seven parts and 625 lines in ballad form, narrating the supernatural voyage of an Ancient Mariner who shoots an albatross (a bird of good omen), brings a curse upon his ship, watches his shipmates die, and is condemned to wander the world, compelled to retell his tale.

The Rime of the Ancient Mariner is a poem of sin, guilt, and penance — the shooting of the albatross is the Mariner's transgression against the natural order; the curse that follows (dead calm, the spectral ship, Death and Life-in-Death playing dice for the crew, the dead men's eyes staring) is the supernatural punishment; and the Mariner's penance is both the physical suffering on the becalmed ship and the perpetual compulsion to retell his story. The poem contains one of the most famous lines in English literature ('Water, water, every where, / Nor any drop to drink') and the moral conclusion that divine love extends to all natural creatures ('He prayeth best, who loveth best / All things both great and small'). The marginal gloss added in 1817 creates an ironic commentary on the narrative, complicating the poem's meaning.

The Rime of the Ancient Mariner is the foundational text of English Romanticism's engagement with the sublime and the supernatural — with the terrifying, transgressive power of nature and the supernatural, the psychology of guilt and obsession, and the power of storytelling itself. Its influence extended to Romantic poetry, Gothic fiction, and the broader cultural fascination with sea voyages, obsession, and the uncanny.","subjects":["English Literature","Early Modern Era","Samuel Taylor Coleridge","Romantic Poetry","Supernatural","Guilt and Redemption","Maritime Literature","Lyrical Ballads"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"This poem has had a lasting impact on the literary canon, contributing significantly to the Romantic movement and shaping the way writers approach themes of nature, morality, and the human condition.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
